WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:05.559
HBO's Watchmen forges its own path, but it
also doesn't forget where it came from.

00:00:05.559 --> 00:00:10.520
It's packed full of covert references throughout
its first season - so if you're curious, here

00:00:10.520 --> 00:00:12.800
are some of the Easter eggs you might have
missed.

00:00:13.360 --> 00:00:18.720
In what might be a meta-joke about Easter
eggs, Watchmen features a ton of actual eggs.

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:23.360
"Which came first, the chicken or the egg?

00:00:23.360 --> 00:00:25.830
The answer appears to be both."

00:00:25.830 --> 00:00:28.270
Some serve as references to the comic series.

00:00:28.270 --> 00:00:33.079
For example, in the first episode, as Angela
Abar breaks up egg yolks in a bowl, they form

00:00:33.079 --> 00:00:37.559
the smiley face you might recognize from the
cover of the first issue of Watchmen.

00:00:37.559 --> 00:00:41.530
There are plenty of other eggs throughout
the season that are more generally symbolic.

00:00:41.530 --> 00:00:45.659
Dr. Manhattan conjures and drinks one during
his first date with Angela.

00:00:45.659 --> 00:00:49.620
In the fourth episode, we see a couple that
gets some much-needed fertility help from

00:00:49.620 --> 00:00:53.050
the mysterious Lady Trieu spilling eggs on
their porch.

00:00:53.050 --> 00:00:57.480
And during the series finale, Angela finds
one egg left unbroken among the others she

00:00:57.480 --> 00:00:59.850
smashed to the ground earlier in the evening.

00:00:59.850 --> 00:01:05.110
Dr. Manhattan left that egg behind after his
death as a way of transferring his powers.

00:01:05.120 --> 00:01:09.640
Angela consumes the egg, but whether she has
his powers is left ambiguous.

00:01:09.760 --> 00:01:14.640
Let's jump back to the smiley face image:
Naturally, show creator Damon Lindelof and

00:01:14.650 --> 00:01:19.640
his crew found other ways to work the iconic
visual into the show, particularly the blood

00:01:19.640 --> 00:01:21.570
spatter on the Comedian's yellow button.

00:01:21.570 --> 00:01:27.070
The first episode of Watchmen opens on a horrific
depiction of the Tulsa Race Riot of 1921,

00:01:27.070 --> 00:01:31.950
which remains one of the most devastating
instances of racial violence in U.S. history.

00:01:31.950 --> 00:01:36.570
White citizens attacked Greenwood, a primarily
African-American neighborhood, looting, robbing,

00:01:36.570 --> 00:01:38.480
and killing with abandon.

00:01:38.480 --> 00:01:43.050
In Watchmen's universe, descendants of the
attack were paid reparations, causing resentment

00:01:43.050 --> 00:01:44.670
among white supremacists.

00:01:44.670 --> 00:01:49.321
In that sequence, the show sneaks in a terrible,
tragic reminder of the button - a blood spatter

00:01:49.321 --> 00:01:53.370
on the head of young Will Reeves that perfectly
mimics the smiley face.

00:01:53.370 --> 00:01:57.659
A similar blood splatter can be spotted later
in the same episode, on Judd's badge during

00:01:57.659 --> 00:01:59.040
his hanging.

00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:03.560
Watchmen takes place in a world quite different
from ours, where Nixon never resigned and

00:02:03.560 --> 00:02:05.950
Robert Redford was the president that followed
him.

00:02:05.950 --> 00:02:10.229
A lot is different culturally, too, including
the popular reading material.

00:02:10.229 --> 00:02:14.670
Max Shea's pirate yarn Tales of the Black
Freighter is prominently featured and excerpted

00:02:14.670 --> 00:02:16.139
in the original comic.

00:02:16.139 --> 00:02:20.720
That explains why there are so many references
to pirates in the show, specifically, a detective

00:02:20.720 --> 00:02:25.590
named Pirate Jenny, a costume donned by one
of Angela's children, a skull-and-crossbones

00:02:25.590 --> 00:02:31.139
wax seal on a game warden's letter, a pirate
flag, the name of Laurie's hotel, and more.

00:02:31.139 --> 00:02:35.870
Several characters are also spotted reading
a book called Fogdancing in quick blink-and-you'll-miss-it

00:02:35.870 --> 00:02:39.650
shots, another novel written by the prolific,
fictional Shea.

00:02:39.650 --> 00:02:40.650
Seems like it's pretty good, too.

00:02:40.650 --> 00:02:42.780
"Would you like me to get you another book?

00:02:42.780 --> 00:02:45.420
"No, I like this one.”

00:02:45.420 --> 00:02:49.700
When a young Angela looks at a series of VHS
tapes in the show's seventh episode, one of

00:02:49.700 --> 00:02:52.600
them is a Hollywood adaptation of Fogdancing.

00:02:52.880 --> 00:02:57.480
Both versions of Nite Owl were major characters
in the Watchmen comic, but his story doesn't

00:02:57.499 --> 00:02:59.040
get much play in the show.

00:02:59.040 --> 00:03:03.900
The show is, however, rife with actual owls
and references to the nocturnal birds.

00:03:03.900 --> 00:03:05.160
Laurie even has a pet owl.

00:03:05.160 --> 00:03:06.800
"What's his name?"

00:03:06.800 --> 00:03:07.960
"Who."

00:03:07.960 --> 00:03:08.960
"The owl."

00:03:10.160 --> 00:03:11.360
"Who.

00:03:11.360 --> 00:03:12.160
That's his name.

00:03:12.160 --> 00:03:13.000
Ask him yourself."

00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:17.639
Angela's other daughter dresses as an owl,
and the kids also have a few owl toys visible

00:03:17.639 --> 00:03:18.639
on their dresser.

00:03:18.639 --> 00:03:23.590
Plus, when Angela dons night-vision goggles,
she looks eerily like Nite Owl himself.

00:03:23.590 --> 00:03:27.959
The first Nite Owl's famous tell-all book,
Under the Hood, also makes a cameo.

00:03:27.959 --> 00:03:32.150
In the first episode, a copy of the book is
on doomed Sheriff Judd Nelson's desk when

00:03:32.150 --> 00:03:37.319
Angela stops by his office, and during their
subsequent chat, she sips coffee from an owl-shaped

00:03:37.320 --> 00:03:38.320
mug.

00:03:38.440 --> 00:03:42.680
One of the big questions in the early episodes
of the season was where Dr. Manhattan was

00:03:42.689 --> 00:03:43.689
hiding.

00:03:43.689 --> 00:03:45.599
Was he on Mars as widely believed?

00:03:45.599 --> 00:03:46.769
Or somewhere else?

00:03:46.769 --> 00:03:51.640
As it turned out, he was hiding in plain sight
as Cal Abar, supportive, loving husband to

00:03:51.640 --> 00:03:52.520
Angela.

00:03:52.520 --> 00:03:56.800
After the two fell in love, Dr. Manhattan
took on the appearance of a regular human

00:03:56.829 --> 00:04:01.200
and had Angela wipe his memory so he could
live as normal a life as possible, at least

00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:02.359
for a while.

00:04:02.359 --> 00:04:05.189
There are plenty of clues leading up to Cal's
big reveal.

00:04:05.200 --> 00:04:09.120
Other characters are skeptical about Cal's
"accident" that left him with amnesia.

00:04:09.120 --> 00:04:11.720
"It was a car crash, right?

00:04:11.730 --> 00:04:15.189
I've done a lot of research on memory.

00:04:15.200 --> 00:04:17.560
Total amnesia's extremely rare."

00:04:17.560 --> 00:04:22.440
And he and Laurie, a former lover of Dr. Manhattan,
seem to have a special connection right off

00:04:22.450 --> 00:04:23.450
the bat.

00:04:23.450 --> 00:04:26.160
In the eighth episode, we learn how he and
Angela met.

00:04:26.160 --> 00:04:31.130
Two very specific musical cues accompany Dr.
Manhattan throughout the episode: "Mr. Blue"

00:04:31.130 --> 00:04:34.600
by The Fleetwoods and a riff on George Gershwin's
"Rhapsody in Blue."

00:04:35.080 --> 00:04:40.480
In the Watchmen universe, the U.S. wins the
Vietnam War decisively thanks to Dr. Manhattan's

00:04:40.480 --> 00:04:43.980
help, and President Richard Nixon is exalted
as a hero.

00:04:43.980 --> 00:04:48.400
Vietnam becomes the 51st state, and Nixon
is remembered as an American icon.

00:04:48.400 --> 00:04:53.120
Throughout the series, Vietnam's inclusion
in the United States is addressed rather broadly.

00:04:53.120 --> 00:04:57.280
Angela grows up there, and Saigon is mentioned
repeatedly as an American city.

00:04:57.280 --> 00:05:01.290
During Judd Crawford's funeral, the flag draped
over his coffin looks very different from

00:05:01.290 --> 00:05:04.340
the real flag, and it bears 51 stars.

00:05:04.340 --> 00:05:08.210
During the show's pilot, when Looking Glass
interrogates a suspect inside a mysterious

00:05:08.210 --> 00:05:13.350
chamber that shows alternating patriotic and
disturbing images, Nixon's face can be seen

00:05:13.360 --> 00:05:15.360
on a revised Mount Rushmore.

00:05:15.640 --> 00:05:20.880
The bar in Saigon where Angela and Dr. Manhattan
first meet is called Mr. Eddy's Bar, a direct

00:05:20.880 --> 00:05:24.780
reference to the comics and the site of one
of its most horrific events.

00:05:24.780 --> 00:05:29.010
Eddie Blake, also known as the Comedian, took
up with a Vietnamese woman during the war,

00:05:29.010 --> 00:05:32.000
but when she became pregnant, he lost interest
in her.

00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:36.180
After the unnamed woman slashed Blake's face
with a beer bottle, leaving a scar, he shot

00:05:36.180 --> 00:05:38.680
her in the face at Mr. Eddy's and killed her.

00:05:38.680 --> 00:05:42.920
The director of the episode, Nicole Kassell,
told Polygon that the show's production crew

00:05:42.920 --> 00:05:47.280
used descriptions from the comic to inform
what the interior of Mr. Eddy's would look

00:05:47.280 --> 00:05:48.280
like.

00:05:48.280 --> 00:05:52.430
Dr. Manhattan explains towards the end of
the season that everything that will happen

00:05:52.430 --> 00:05:57.330
has either already occurred or is happening
right now, making every outcome inevitable.

00:05:57.330 --> 00:05:59.760
He's also notably the son of a watchmaker.

00:05:59.760 --> 00:06:04.040
As such, there's a clock motif that runs through
Watchmen, connecting Dr. Manhattan's thought

00:06:04.040 --> 00:06:06.500
process and wisdom to Adrian Veidt.

00:06:06.500 --> 00:06:11.351
Veidt, who spends years imprisoned on Europa
with cloned servants created by Dr. Manhattan

00:06:11.351 --> 00:06:15.800
as his only company, writes a play for the
clones to perform called The Watchmaker's

00:06:15.800 --> 00:06:16.800
Son.

00:06:16.800 --> 00:06:20.360
It's a retelling of Dr. Manhattan's origin
story, though it burns one of the clones to

00:06:20.360 --> 00:06:22.870
death rather than giving him superpowers.

00:06:22.870 --> 00:06:26.530
Beyond that, there are plenty of clocks in
the show, all of which reference the comics'

00:06:26.530 --> 00:06:31.820
Doomsday Clock as well as its final cover,
which itself depicts a bloody clock.

